iroh is pancake's encrypted transport, and the one that lets two peers reach each
other without a server in the middle. A session's bytes ride
[QUIC](https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9000.html) carried by
[iroh](https://www.iroh.computer/), and a peer is named by a self-authenticating
key rather than a `host:port`. Everything above the wire stays the same OCapN
pancake speaks over TCP, so switching to iroh changes only two things: how a peer
is addressed, and that its channel is now encrypted and authenticated end to end.

## One key does three jobs

Each endpoint is an Ed25519 keypair, and pancake makes that single key carry three
roles at once:

- it is the **iroh node id** the transport authenticates the QUIC channel against;
- it is the **designator**, the peer's name inside an OCapN locator, the part
  you dial in an `ocapn://<node-id>.iroh` URI;
- it is the **session signing key** that signs the `op:start-session` handshake.

[One keypair fills all three roles, so the peer you dialed is provably the peer that signed the session.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-iroh-key.svg'))

The session key, authenticated node id, and signed locator designator must all
match. There is no separate certificate or host address to trust.

Binding from the same seed restores the node identity at a new network
address. A sturdyref works after that restart only if the application also
restores the named object under the same swiss number.

## Reaching a peer

A peer hands you its node id; how that id turns into a route is a *reachability
posture* you choose when you bind the endpoint. pancake exposes four:

| Posture | How a dial resolves |
| --- | --- |
| `direct` | Locator hints carry the peer's socket addresses |
| `local_discovery` | An in-process address book resolves the id |
| `discovery` | Public n0 relays and DNS/pkarr resolve the id |
| `discovery_no_relay` | DNS/pkarr resolves published addresses with relay fallback disabled |

`direct` and `local_discovery` are deterministic. `discovery` publishes node
information and depends on public infrastructure and propagation. The
no-relay form needs a publicly reachable direct address.

## A separate artifact, by design

iroh's QUIC stack is Rust, so it rides a sidecar staticlib that pancake keeps in
its own compiled artifact, `libpancake_net_transport_iroh`
([ADR-0055](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0055-iroh-netlayer-sidecar.md)).
The base archives stay pure Zig and dependency-free; a program links iroh only
when it wants it. Building the companion archive requires `cargo`.

The same split shows up in the bindings. Each one keeps iroh in a per-transport
module: the `@pancake/iroh` package, `pancake.iroh`, `pancake-iroh.rkt`, and Rust's
opt-in `iroh` feature. `connect` loads that module lazily the first time it sees an
`iroh` locator (Rust compiles it in behind the feature instead), so a program that
only ever speaks TCP never loads it. Hosting over iroh adds one verb, a
listen-over-iroh call each binding names its own way (`listenIroh` in TypeScript,
`listen_iroh` in Python). Dialing a peer over iroh goes through the same `connect`
as TCP; the only difference a caller sees is the address.

## Scale: many connections, one thread

The blocking path uses one OS thread per connection. To serve many, a loop host
drives every connection from one
readiness descriptor
([ADR-0057](https://github.com/jimisaacs/pancake/blob/main/docs/decisions/adr/ADR-0057-iroh-completion-loop-bridge.md)):
[Performance]($link.page('performance')) measures the serial and readiness-loop
paths. The sidecar heap is separate from pancake's core session state.

## Three-party handoff over iroh

A handoff over iroh is the same introduction as over any other session
([Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) covers the shape): the gifter names the
exporter by node id, and the recipient redeems by dialing that id on a fresh
encrypted session.

For a full application, run the [rooms]($link.page('examples')) chat with
`zig build demo-rooms-iroh`. [What you can depend on]($link.page('stability'))
separates deterministic checks from public discovery tests.
